responding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-6x},$ and ${e}^{-6x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-6\\alpha {e}^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha {e}^{-6x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{-6x}) & =12{e}^{-6x} \\\\ (-6-6)\\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =12{e}^{-6x} \\\\ -12\\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =12{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6x}-{e}^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-7$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1 & =-7 \\\\ A & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-6{e}^{6x}-{e}^{-6x}.\n$$" 13194139538888 17592186045443 true] [17592186673928 1080 "-\\frac{57}{7}{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{5x}" 13194139538888 17592186045443 true] [17592186673928 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538888 17592186045443 true] [17592186673928 1083 #uuid "6a4eb57b-e6ba-5ef5-821a-9e8223d3da39" 13194139538888 17592186045443 true] [17592186673929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186673932 13194139538888 17592186045443 true] [17592186673929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538888 17592186045443 true] [17592186673929 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=6{e}^{4x},\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=6{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139538888 17592186045443 true] [17592186673929 1072 #uuid "6c3a86d8-ae5c-514d-924c-56a2a4c4c7d1" 13194139538888 17592186045443 true] [17592186673929 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4\\alpha {e}^{4x})+5(\\alpha {e}^{4x}) & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ (4+5)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ 9\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=5$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+\\frac{2}{3} & =5 \\\\ A & =\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{13}{3}{e}^{-5x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$" 13194139538888 17592186045443 true] [17592186673930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186673935 13194139538888 17592186045443 true] [17592186673930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538888 17592186045443 true] [17592186673930 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=10{e}^{3x},\\,y(0)=-6\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=10{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139538888 17592186045443 true] [17592186673930 1072 #uuid "7751656d-de90-5f27-a82a-a3ddc66b626a" 13194139538888 17592186045443 true] [17592186673930 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $10{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=3\\alpha {e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})-4(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =10{e}^{3x} \\\\ (3-4)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =10{e}^{3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =10{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alp